# manual-checks.allowlist -- check scripts that legitimately CANNOT run in
# check-contracts.sh, each with the reason.
#
# UNWIRED-CHECK-CLOSE-1 (2026-07-30). Enforced by check-contract-coverage.py:
# every scripts/check-*.{py,sh} must be either wired into check-contracts.sh
# or listed here WITH A REASON. A row with no reason fails the coverage check,
# because an unexplained exemption is indistinguishable from an oversight --
# and an oversight here is exactly how 21 checks went dark and 6 of them sat
# red for weeks with nobody watching.
#
# Format:  <script-name>: <reason it cannot run in the seconds-scale aggregator>
#
# Being on this list is NOT permission to rot. Each row states what would have
# to change for the script to become wirable, so the exemption is falsifiable.

# --- needs a compiler / build ---------------------------------------------

# --- needs a cooked asset tree --------------------------------------------

# --- needs a deployed release tree ----------------------------------------
check-ab-kit.py: Grades the A/B batch kit inside a DEPLOYED INSTALL, supplied as its one positional argument (DST = sys.argv[1]). Nothing it reads is in the repo -- no AB-*.bat is tracked on any branch, so a clone has nothing for it to parse and BOTH entry conditions raise: IndexError on sys.argv[1] with no argument, and IndexError on the AB-0 baseline lookup against a directory with no kit. Wiring it means hard-coding an install name that encodes the day it was cut (A:/Games/MC2-OpenGL-FINAL-2026-07-31 today), and that row is red on every clone forever and red on this disk the moment the directory is renamed or superseded -- the permanently-red wallpaper this file already refuses twice (check-flag-registry-drift.sh, check-env-allowlist.sh). The obvious escape, skip-when-absent, is worse: it is structurally GREEN in every clone, i.e. a report wearing a gate's colour, which is exactly what DELIVERY-CLOSE-1 refused for check-delivery.py's install half. MEASURED 2026-07-31 so the exemption is falsifiable rather than assumed -- `py -3 scripts/check-ab-kit.py "A:/Games/MC2-OpenGL-FINAL-2026-07-31"` reports `9 arms checked, 0 failing` and `common block: 9/9 arms byte-identical, 0 failing`, exit 0; injection-proven to exit 1 in RECORD-TRUTH-1 (an arm's set line edited to contradict its own filename). ABKIT-BYTEIDENTITY-1 (2026-07-31) added the second half, and it does NOT change this exemption's reasoning: the byte-identity assertion also reads AB-*.bat from DST, and it runs AFTER the AB-0 baseline lookup, so both entry conditions above still raise on a clone exactly as described -- the new check adds a third install-dependent read rather than a clone-satisfiable one. It is injection-proven too, against the near-miss the effective-value replay is structurally blind to: a global substitution restoring an injected arm rewrites BOTH copies of a gate (the common default line and the override), leaving every effective value correct while the common block diverges -- old checker exit 0, new checker exit 1 with a unified diff at the offending line. The shipped copy at <install>/verify-ab-kit.py is byte-identical to this one (sha256 d726abad8768114dbdd022f7c9f141343fafd378a98c86d36968770950d7cafa), so grading the install grades this file. KIT-COHERENCE-1 (2026-07-31) made that last sentence MACHINE-CHECKED rather than a hash typed into a prose row: scripts/kit-digests.csv carries the same digest with source=scripts/check-ab-kit.py, and scripts/check-deploy-coherence.py (which IS wired, via run_smoke.py) now fails if the repo copy moves without being re-shipped, or if any of the thirteen kit files drifts. That closes the OTHER blindness this row could not: check-ab-kit.py grades what the arms SAY, and is structurally blind to a byte changed in an arm's header prose -- above the `cd /d` anchor, not a `set` line -- e.g. the measured "47.7% brighter / 48.0% darker" a reader uses to judge the arm. Injection-proven 2026-07-31: that one-byte edit left `9 arms checked, 0 failing` / `9/9 byte-identical` (exit 0) here AND `OK: matches manifest` (exit 0) in the pre-change coherence checker, because the install's .deployed_manifest.csv covered 805 payload files and none of the 13 kit files -- they were written in after deploy_payload ran. Post-change: `kit hash drift` and exit 1 under --strict. NOTE this does NOT move the exemption below: kit-digests.csv is a drift PIN, not a source -- a clone still has no AB-*.bat to parse, so both entry conditions still raise. TRIGGER for wiring: the kit stops being hand-written into an install and becomes a GENERATED artifact (an emitter under scripts/ or tools/ that deploy_payload.py drives), at which point the input tree is in-repo, clone-independent and wirable against the generator's own output; or a deploy-lane runner that owns exactly one install by contract.

